 \subsection{Limitations and Future Work}\label{sec:limitations}
 
- The correction terms are identified for this test stand, these knee configurations, and these actuator families. Other joints or actuator types should repeat the two-stage procedure rather than reuse the values. That procedure is inexpensive next to the test campaign it saves, which is the point of having it.
-
- Isometric torque testing has yielded important design factors for roboticists seeking to design biomimetic humanoid robots. Additional testing such as isokinetic and isotonic that are frequently used in in human dynamometer testing may yield equally as important insights.
+ The correction terms are identified for this test stand, these knee configurations, and these actuator families. Other joints or actuator types should repeat the two-stage procedure rather than reuse the values. Transfer to other systems requires validation against their measured torque.
+
+ Isometric torque testing has yielded important design factors for roboticists seeking to design biomimetic humanoid robots. Isokinetic and isotonic tests would assess behavior outside the isometric conditions evaluated here.
 
 \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
 
-This work started with an improved braided pneumatic actuator force-length-pressure model and actually delivers about a joint, and answered with a corrected torque model and the design workflow built on it. Isometric knee-torque measurements on pinned and biomimetic test stands, interpreted through a hybrid calculation that substitutes the measured muscle length and moment arm, isolated the error sources that rigid-body torque models miss: attachment compliance, a constant effective-length offset, and a wrapping loss that acts like fictive strain, expressed through the unloaded \qty{620}{\kPa} contraction of the free actuator. A two-stage identification recovered the correction terms with one stiffness pair shared across the flexor and extensor configurations, roughly halving fitting-set error, cutting held-out error by two thirds or more, and carrying the corrected model from the pinned knee to the biomimetic knee without refitting. The attachment routes themselves were then redesigned under the corrected model: two $\diameter$\qty{20}{\mm} actuators per route, graded against OpenSim human isometric torque profiles with a required \qty{5}{\percent} overshoot, meet or exceed the human flexor and extensor envelopes at every angle, the cam extensor design converts the thinnest margins into design headroom, the doctrine re-solves quantify how a reeved tackle, fed by parallel actuator pairs, converts surplus force into range of motion across five muscle sites, and the redesigned extensor route's physical validation spans nine data measurements from deep flexion to extension that meet or exceed the human target at every measured angle. The design-factor reading is the practical summary: moment arm, normalized contraction, and path compliance are the three quantities a designer of BPA-actuated joints controls, and each now carries a measurable, correctable model. First-iteration torque design for such joints is, as a result, no longer a guess.
+Isometric knee-torque measurements on pinned and biomimetic test stands showed that actuator force characterization alone does not determine delivered joint torque. A hybrid calculation using measured muscle length and moment arm helped separate force-model error from routing and attachment effects. The corrected torque model represents a constant length offset, effective attachment compliance, and an empirical wrapping loss. A two-stage identification shares one stiffness pair across flexor and extensor configurations and reduces prediction error on the held-out tests.
+
+Route redesign under the corrected model produces two-\qty{20}{\mm}-BPA configurations that meet the selected human-muscle torque targets over their design grids, with the required \qty{5}{\percent} margin. The cam extensor increases predicted torque headroom while reducing the required operating pressure. Physical tests meet or exceed the respective human targets at all 15 measured flexor points and all nine measured extensor points. These measurements validate the tested isometric configurations; dynamic loading and transfer to other joints remain to be evaluated.
 
